  "decision": {
    "answer": "NEI",
    "explanation": "The provided evidence from pmid-27009859 focuses exclusively on resistance mutations in the EML4-ALK fusion gene, specifically highlighting the I117S (canonical I1171S) mutation in the context of ALK inhibitor resistance. The evidence does not mention FGFR3, the S459I variant, or any functional characterization of FGFR3 mutations. Therefore, there is insufficient evidence to support, revise, or refute the claim regarding FGFR3 S459I being a gain-of-function rare germline variant.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-27009859",
        "start": 8,
        "end": 608,
        "quote": "IC50s of each resistant line compared to the parent line from which they were derived . . . Interestingly, cross-validation using the lung cancer-derived EML4-ALK fusion showed both similarities and key differences in the effect of mutations on particular drugs. Our study therefore characterizes novel NPM-ALK TKI resistance mutations, . . . Consistent with its original identification in EML4-ALK-driven systems, we find I1171S is potently resistance-conferring to FL5.12 cells dependent on this fusion. All six TKIs tested had significantly increased IC50s when tested vs. EML4-ALK-I117S compared "
      },
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-27009859",
        "start": 608,
        "end": 828,
        "quote": "to wild-type . . . Several other amino acid substitutions occur at I1171 residue, all promoting resistance to both crizotinib and alectinib regardless of whether their characterization was in the context of NSCLC or ALCL"
      }
    ],
